# myproject

A library for parsing and emitting Foo records.

## Installation

```sh
cargo add myproject
```

## Quick start

The simplest way to read a Foo file:

```rs
use myproject::Foo;

fn main() {
    let foo = Foo::from_path("input.foo").unwrap();
    println!("{}", foo.summary());
}
```

This works for all Foo records that comply with the v2 spec. For older v1
records, you need the `legacy` feature flag enabled. Records that span
multiple files are also supported — see the multi-part guide below.

## Multi-part records

Multi-part records are split across N files with the `.part-N.foo` suffix.
The reader stitches them back together automatically:

```rs
let foo = Foo::from_path("input.part-1.foo").unwrap();
assert_eq!(foo.part_count(), 3);
```

## Configuration

| Key | Default | Description |
| --- | ------- | ----------- |
| `max_records` | 10_000 | Maximum records per file |
| `strict_mode` | true | Reject non-spec records |
| `encoding` | utf-8 | Input file encoding |

### Advanced tuning

For high-throughput pipelines, increase `max_records` and disable
`strict_mode`. See the performance guide for the tradeoffs involved.

